**Proof.** We prove the identity by comparing $p$-adic valuations for an arbitrary prime $p$.

**Left-hand side.** By Legendre's formula,
$$v_p(n!) = \sum_{k=1}^{\infty}\left\lfloor \frac{n}{p^k}\right\rfloor.$$

**Right-hand side.** We compute
$$v_p\!\left(\prod_{i=1}^{n}\operatorname{lcm}\{1,2,\dots,\lfloor n/i\rfloor\}\right)=\sum_{i=1}^{n}v_p\!\left(\operatorname{lcm}\{1,2,\dots,\lfloor n/i\rfloor\}\right).$$

For any integer $m\ge 1$, the $p$-adic valuation of $\operatorname{lcm}\{1,2,\dots,m\}$ is the largest exponent $k$ such that $p^k\le m$; equivalently
$$v_p\!\left(\operatorname{lcm}\{1,\dots,m\}\right)=\sum_{k=1}^{\infty}\mathbf{1}\!\left[p^k\le m\right].$$
(Note that for $1\le i\le n$ we have $\lfloor n/i\rfloor\ge 1$, so this applies to every factor.)

Substituting $m=\lfloor n/i\rfloor$ and exchanging the order of summation,
$$\sum_{i=1}^{n}v_p\!\left(\operatorname{lcm}\{1,\dots,\lfloor n/i\rfloor\}\right)=\sum_{k=1}^{\infty}\#\Bigl\{i\in\{1,\dots,n\}:p^k\le \lfloor n/i\rfloor\Bigr\}.$$

Now observe that for a positive integer $i$,
$$p^k\le \lfloor n/i\rfloor \iff p^k\le n/i \iff i\le n/p^k.$$
Hence the count of such $i$ is exactly $\lfloor n/p^k\rfloor$, and therefore
$$\sum_{i=1}^{n}v_p\!\left(\operatorname{lcm}\{1,\dots,\lfloor n/i\rfloor\}\right)=\sum_{k=1}^{\infty}\left\lfloor \frac{n}{p^k}\right\rfloor=v_p(n!).$$

**Conclusion.** For every prime $p$, both sides have the same $p$-adic valuation:
$$v_p\!\left(\prod_{i=1}^{n}\operatorname{lcm}\{1,\dots,\lfloor n/i\rfloor\}\right)=v_p(n!).$$
Since this holds for all primes and both sides are positive integers, the two sides are equal:
$$n!=\prod_{i=1}^{n}\operatorname{lcm}\{1,2,\dots,\lfloor n/i\rfloor\}. \qquad\blacksquare$$

